## Recap & What’s Ahead

In [**Part One**](https://medium.com/deloitte-uk-cloud-blog/a-guide-to-generating-swift-code-with-swift-mustache-part-1-d985711e2c2c), we explored the foundations of Swift code generation, covering how templating with **Swift Mustache** can help generate simple Swift structs from JSON Schema.

Now in **Part Two**, we’ll level up with more powerful tools: **SwiftSyntax**, which provides deep control over Swift’s abstract syntax trees, and **Swift OpenAPI Generator**, which lets you generate Swift code from OpenAPI specs.

Keep Umm Kulthum [playing in the background](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=VsZReSJ5AU8), and let’s dive in! 🎶

## Approach 2: Swift Syntax

> [SwiftSyntax](https://github.com/apple/swift-syntax) is a powerful tool that enables developers to programmatically generate Swift source code by building syntax trees.

### What is a Syntax Tree?

A Syntax Tree, or an Abstract Syntax Tree (AST), is a structured representation of Swift source code. It breaks the code into smaller syntax nodes, each representing a fundamental part of the Swift language, such as keywords, expressions, statements, and declarations.

Think of it as Swift code turned into a Lego set: each piece (node) fits into a specific spot to form the complete picture.

### Defining Swift Code Using a Syntax Tree

To visualize this, try the [Swift AST Explorer](https://swift-ast-explorer.com/):

1- Open the tool.

2- Type a simple Swift struct like this:

```
struct User {
    let id: Int
    let name: String
}
```

The **Swift AST Explorer** will generate a structured representation of this code, showing how Swift breaks it down into syntax nodes.

```
SourceFile
 ├─ StructDecl (struct User)
 │   ├─ Identifier (User)
 │   ├─ MemberDeclBlock
 │       ├─ VariableDecl (let id: Int)
 │       ├─ VariableDecl (let name: String)
```

Each node corresponds to a part of the code, and SwiftSyntax allows you to build, modify, and manipulate these nodes.

To visualize this, try the [Swift AST Explorer:](https://swift-ast-explorer.com/)

Press enter or click to view image in full size

![](https://miro.medium.com/v2/resize:fit:700/1*BhY76X8W_H3XIQSZK4DPzQ.png)

### How Does SwiftSyntax Work?

Instead of using string-based templates like Mustache. SwiftSyntax offers an accurate tree-based model of Swift source code, empowering Swift tools to parse, inspect, generate, and transform Swift source code using structured syntax defined in [SwiftSyntax](https://github.com/apple/swift-syntax/tree/main/Sources/SwiftSyntax) and [SwiftSyntaxBuilder](https://github.com/apple/swift-syntax/tree/main/Sources/SwiftSyntaxBuilder), with support from Swift [AST](https://swift-ast-explorer.com/) Explorer.

**Example Generating a Struct with SwiftSyntax**

```
func generateStruct(name: String, properties: [(String, String)]) -> DeclSyntax {
    let propertiesSyntax = properties.map { "let \($0): \($1)" }.joined(separator: "\n")
    return DeclSyntax("""
        struct \(name): Codable {
            \(propertiesSyntax)
        }
    """)
}
```

**Generated Swift Code**

```
struct Person: Codable {
    let name: String
    let age: Int
}
```

### Integrating SwiftSyntax in [Your Project](https://github.com/esraaeiid/SwiftCodeGenerator)

Now that we understand the basics, let’s implement it within the [SwiftCodeGenerator](https://github.com/esraaeiid/SwiftCodeGenerator/tree/main/Sources) repository:

1\. Add SwiftSyntax Dependency

In `Package.swift`:

```
let package = Package(
   name: "SwiftGenerator",
   platforms: [\
     .macOS(.v12)\
 ],
   products: [\
    .executable(\
            name: "SwiftGeneratorExecutable",\
            targets: ["SwiftGeneratorExecutable"]\
        ),\
\
   .library(\
        name: "SwiftGenerator",\
        targets: ["SwiftGenerator"]),\
   ],
   dependencies: [\
 // Swift Syntax: Apple's official library for parsing and generating Swift code\
    .package(url: "https://github.com/hummingbird-project/hummingbird-mustache.git", from: "1.0.3"),\
    .package(url: "https://github.com/apple/swift-syntax.git", from: "510.0.2"),\
   ],
   targets: [\
    .target(name: "SwiftGenerator" , dependencies: [\
        .product(name: "HummingbirdMustache", package: "hummingbird-mustache"),\
// Swift code parsing and generation\
        .product(name: "SwiftSyntax", package: "swift-syntax"),\
        .product(name: "SwiftSyntaxBuilder", package: "swift-syntax"),\
    ]),\
    .executableTarget(\
        name: "SwiftGeneratorExecutable",\
        dependencies: [\
            .byName(name: "SwiftGenerator"),\
        ]\
    ),\
    .testTarget(\
        name: "SwiftGeneratorTests",\
        dependencies: ["SwiftGenerator"]\
    ),\
   ]
)
```

2\. Create `SwiftSyntaxGenerator.swift`

In `Sources/SwiftGenerator/SwiftSyntax`, create `SwiftSyntaxGenerator.swift`

This file will contain the logic responsible for building Swift code using **SwiftSyntax**, which allows us to programmatically define Swift struct (like struct Person) by assembling syntax components instead of writing raw strings. Paste the following code into it:

```
import SwiftSyntax
import SwiftSyntaxBuilder
import Foundation
public enum SwiftSyntaxGenerator {
   static func generateStructSyntax(name: String, properties: [(String, String)]) -> DeclSyntax {
    let structDecl = StructDeclSyntax(
        modifiers: DeclModifierListSyntax {
            DeclModifierSyntax(name: .keyword(.public)) // Marks the struct as public
        },
        name: TokenSyntax(stringLiteral: name), // The name of the struct
        inheritanceClause: InheritanceClauseSyntax {
            InheritedTypeSyntax(type: TypeSyntax("Codable")) // Adds Codable conformance
        }
    ) {
        for (propertyName, propertyType) in properties {
            VariableDeclSyntax(bindingSpecifier: .keyword(.let)) { // Declares a constant property
                PatternBindingSyntax(
                    pattern: IdentifierPatternSyntax(identifier: .identifier(propertyName)), // Property name
                    typeAnnotation: TypeAnnotationSyntax(type: IdentifierTypeSyntax(name: .identifier(propertyType))) // Property type
                )
            }
        }
    }
    .with(\.leadingTrivia, .newlines(1)) // Adds a newline before the
    .with(\.trailingTrivia, .newlines(1)) // Adds a newline after the

    return DeclSyntax(structDecl)
   }
}
```

If you’re unfamiliar with syntax trees or want to understand what these building blocks represent, you can explore the [Swift AST Explorer](https://swift-ast-explorer.com/) or check out the [SwiftSyntax documentation.](https://github.com/apple/swift-syntax)

In the `SwiftSyntaxGenerator.swift` file, add a new function to trigger SwiftSyntax-based generation:

```
public struct SwiftGenerator {
   public init() {}

   //MARK: - Swift Syntax
   public func generateWithSwiftSyntax() {
           // These values represent the properties of the actual Swift struct to generate
   let generatedSyntax = SwiftSyntaxGenerator.generateStructSyntax(name: "User", properties: [("id", "Int"), ("name", "String")])
    print(generatedSyntax)
   }
}
```

Call the function from the main executable file.

```
import SwiftGenerator
let generator = SwiftGenerator ()
generator.generateWithSwiftSyntax()
```

Then run the project in the terminal using:

```
swift run
```

**Note:** The terminal output may not automatically include spaces. To fix this, **Trivia** has been added to SwiftSyntax to format the structure properly.

```
struct User: Codable {
let id: Int
let name: String
let email: String
}
```

### Let’s now extend our implementation to support optional and array types.

Inside `SwiftSyntaxGenerator.swift`, define a helper function to resolve optional and array inside the struct:

```
// Define a public enum to encapsulate the SwiftSyntax generation logic
public enum SwiftSyntaxGenerator {
 // Main function to generate a Swift struct syntax

   static func generateStructSyntax(name: String, properties: [(String, String)]) -> DeclSyntax {
// Define the structure declaration
    let structDecl = StructDeclSyntax(
// Add a 'public' access modifier
        modifiers: [.init(name: .keyword(.public))],
// Add 'struct' keyword explicitly
        structKeyword: .keyword(.struct),
 // Name of the struct
        name: .identifier(name),
// Add inheritance clause (e.g., Codable)
        inheritanceClause: InheritanceClauseSyntax {
            InheritedTypeListSyntax {
                InheritedTypeSyntax(type: TypeSyntax("Codable"))
            }
           },
// Define the member block of the struct (its properties)
        memberBlock: MemberBlockSyntax {
            for (propertyName, propertyType) in properties {
  // Declare each property as 'let'
                VariableDeclSyntax(bindingSpecifier: .keyword(.let)) {
// Create the binding: name and type
                    PatternBindingSyntax(
                        pattern: IdentifierPatternSyntax(identifier: .identifier(propertyName)),
  // Add the type annotation, resolved dynamically
                        typeAnnotation: TypeAnnotationSyntax(
                            type: resolveType(propertyType)
                        )
                    )
                }
            }
        }
       )
// Return the complete DeclSyntax representing the struct
    return DeclSyntax(structDecl)
   }
```

```
// Helper function to handle optional and array types from string format
private static func resolveType(_ typeString: String) -> TypeSyntax {
// Check if the type ends with '?', indicating an optional
    if typeString.hasSuffix("?") {
        let baseType = String(typeString.dropLast()) // remove '?'
        return TypeSyntax(OptionalTypeSyntax(wrappedType: TypeSyntax(stringLiteral: baseType)))
   // Check if the type starts with '[' and ends with ']', indicating an array
    } else if typeString.hasPrefix("[") && typeString.hasSuffix("]") {
        let elementType = String(typeString.dropFirst().dropLast())
        return TypeSyntax(ArrayTypeSyntax(element: TypeSyntax(stringLiteral: elementType)))
// Otherwise, it's just a regular type (e.g., String, Int)
       } else {
        return TypeSyntax(stringLiteral: typeString)
    }
   }
}
```

In the `SwiftGenerator.swift` file, edit the function to look like the following:

```
//MARK: - Swift Syntax
    public func generateWithSwiftSyntax() {

        let generatedSyntax = SwiftSyntaxGenerator.generateStructSyntax(
            name: "Person",
            properties: [\
                ("name", "String?"),\
                ("age", "[Int]"),\
                ("email", "String")\
            ]
        )
        print(generatedSyntax)
    }
```

`swift run` and Voila! You should see the generated structure printed on the terminal:

```
public struct Person: Codable {
    let name: String?
    let age: [Int]
    let email: String
}
```

**Pro Tip:** To improve readability, instead of printing the output in the terminal, try writing it to a file.

### Writing to a File & Using Structs with SwiftSyntax

To improve readability and reusability, instead of printing the output directly to the terminal, let’s write the generated code to a file.

**Step 1: Define a Property Model for SwiftSyntax:**

In **Sources/SwiftGenerator/Model**, create a new file named `SwiftSyntaxPropert.swift` and add the following:

```
import Foundation
// MARK: - Swift Syntax Property Model
/// Represents a property in a Swift struct with type information
/// This model is used by the SwiftSyntax generator to define struct properties
/// with support for optional types, arrays, and various Swift type mappings
public struct SwiftSyntaxProperty {
    let name: String
    let type: String
    let isOptional: Bool
    let isArray: Bool

    /// Initialize a SwiftSyntaxProperty with basic type information
    /// - Parameters:
    ///   - name: The property name (e.g., "id", "name", "email")
    ///   - type: The Swift type (e.g., "String", "Int", "UUID")
    ///   - isOptional: Whether the property is optional (default: false)
    ///   - isArray: Whether the property is an array (default: false)
    public init(name: String, type: String, isOptional: Bool = false, isArray: Bool = false) {
        self.name = name
        self.type = type
        self.isOptional = isOptional
        self.isArray = isArray
    }

    /// Convenience initializer for optional properties
    /// - Parameters:
    ///   - name: The property name
    ///   - type: The Swift type
    /// - Returns: A SwiftSyntaxProperty with isOptional set to true
    public static func optional(_ name: String, type: String) -> SwiftSyntaxProperty {
        return SwiftSyntaxProperty(name: name, type: type, isOptional: true)
    }

    /// Convenience initializer for array properties
    /// - Parameters:
    ///   - name: The property name
    ///   - type: The Swift type (element type of the array)
    /// - Returns: A SwiftSyntaxProperty with isArray set to true
    public static func array(_ name: String, type: String) -> SwiftSyntaxProperty {
        return SwiftSyntaxProperty(name: name, type: type, isArray: true)
    }

    /// Convenience initializer for optional array properties
    /// - Parameters:
    ///   - name: The property name
    ///   - type: The Swift type (element type of the array)
    /// - Returns: A SwiftSyntaxProperty with both isOptional and isArray set to true
    public static func optionalArray(_ name: String, type: String) -> SwiftSyntaxProperty {
        return SwiftSyntaxProperty(name: name, type: type, isOptional: true, isArray: true)
    }
}
```

This `struct` allows you to express whether a property is optional, an array, or both.

**Step 2: Update**`SwiftSyntaxGenerator.swift`

Next, update the generator logic to use SwiftSyntaxProperty:

- Convert each property into a `VariableDeclSyntax`.

```
/// Generates Swift structs using Apple's SwiftSyntax library
/// This provides programmatic control over Swift code generation
public enum SwiftSyntaxGenerator {

    /// Generates Swift struct syntax from a name and array of properties
    /// - Parameters:
    ///   - name: The name of the struct to generate
    ///   - properties: Array of SwiftSyntaxProperty objects defining the struct properties
    /// - Returns: Generated Swift struct as DeclSyntax
    static func generateStructSyntax(name: String, properties: [SwiftSyntaxProperty]) -> DeclSyntax {
        let structDecl = StructDeclSyntax(
            modifiers: [.init(name: .keyword(.public))],
            structKeyword: .keyword(.struct),
            name: .identifier(name),
            inheritanceClause: InheritanceClauseSyntax {
                InheritedTypeListSyntax {
                    InheritedTypeSyntax(type: TypeSyntax("Codable"))
                }
            },
            memberBlock: MemberBlockSyntax {
                for property in properties {
                    VariableDeclSyntax(bindingSpecifier: .keyword(.let)) {
                        PatternBindingSyntax(
                            pattern: IdentifierPatternSyntax(identifier: .identifier(property.name)),
                            typeAnnotation: TypeAnnotationSyntax(
                                type: resolveType(from: property)
                            )
                        )
                    }
                }
            }
        )
        return DeclSyntax(structDecl)
    }

}
```

- Use a helper method `resolveType()`to handle optional and array types.

```
/// Resolves Swift type from property information
    /// - Parameter property: SwiftSyntaxProperty containing type information
    /// - Returns: TypeSyntax representing the Swift type
    private static func resolveType(from property: SwiftSyntaxProperty) -> TypeSyntax {
        var baseType = property.type

        // Handle array types
        if property.isArray {
            baseType = "[\(baseType)]"
        }

        // Handle optional types
        if property.isOptional {
            return TypeSyntax(OptionalTypeSyntax(wrappedType: TypeSyntax(stringLiteral: baseType)))
        } else {
            return TypeSyntax(stringLiteral: baseType)
        }
    }
```

- Add a method to write the result to a file.

```
/// Generates Swift struct and writes it to a file
    /// - Parameters:
    ///   - name: The name of the struct to generate
    ///   - properties: Array of SwiftSyntaxProperty objects
    ///   - filename: Name of the file to create (defaults to struct name + ".swift")
    ///   - directory: Directory to save the file (defaults to "Generated")
    /// - Throws: File system errors if writing fails
    static func generateStructFile(
        name: String,
        properties: [SwiftSyntaxProperty],
        filename: String? = nil,
        directory: String = "Generated"
    ) throws {
        let generatedSyntax = generateStructSyntax(name: name, properties: properties)
        let swiftCode = generatedSyntax.formatted().description

        let fileManager = FileManager.default
        let currentDirectory = fileManager.currentDirectoryPath
        let outputDirectory = "\(currentDirectory)/\(directory)"

        // Create directory if it doesn't exist
        if !fileManager.fileExists(atPath: outputDirectory) {
            try fileManager.createDirectory(atPath: outputDirectory, withIntermediateDirectories: true)
        }

        // Use provided filename or default to struct name
        let finalFilename = filename ?? "\(name).swift"
        let filePath = "\(outputDirectory)/\(finalFilename)"

        try swiftCode.write(toFile: filePath, atomically: true, encoding: .utf8)
        print("Generated SwiftSyntax file: \(filePath)")
    }


    /// Legacy method for backward compatibility
    /// - Parameters:
    ///   - name: Struct name
    ///   - properties: Array of (name, type) tuples
    /// - Returns: Generated Swift struct as DeclSyntax
    static func generateStructSyntax(name: String, properties: [(String, String)]) -> DeclSyntax {
        // Convert tuple array to SwiftSyntaxProperty array
        let swiftProperties = properties.map { (name, type) in
            SwiftSyntaxProperty(name: name, type: type)
        }
        return generateStructSyntax(name: name, properties: swiftProperties)
    }
```

